## Overview

I built Punters' first design system from scratch in three months. It cleared 1,450+ accessibility errors, lifted designer productivity by 30%, and turned out solid enough to later rebrand an entire sister site, saving close to a month of rework down the line.

Punters is a racing media platform that helps people make smarter betting decisions using stats on horse and greyhound racing. Its core audience, men aged 20 to 40, had used the site since 2014, but it had never run on a unified design system. Ten years of design debt had piled up, and the dev team's move from Nuxt 2 to Nuxt 3 (the framework the site runs on) opened the door to build one properly, in step with the rebuild.

## My role

Lead designer for the design system, with full ownership of the architecture and the build. That covered creating and naming the design tokens, building the tokenised components, setting up the Figma styles and variables, and prototyping the interactive components. I also led adoption across a cross-functional team of 15: designers, developers, and product managers, most of whom had never worked in a token-based system before.

## Problem

**Ten years of a product with no system, and the files showed it.** There was 65 shades of grey in use across the source files alone. I'm not rounding for effect, I counted.

No consistent colour scale, no type system, no unified spacing or grid. Icon styles varied even for the same concept, so the same idea got drawn different ways on different pages. Components were managed separately across four platforms (desktop web, mobile web, iOS, Android), which meant the same button could exist as four unrelated buttons with no shared source.

On top of that, a WAVE audit (a tool that flags accessibility problems) of the six most-visited pages found over 1,450 contrast errors, meaning text and interface elements that were too hard to read. For a site whose whole pitch is being a trusted, data-led source, that debt was quietly undermining the product.

## Goals

- Build a fully tokenised design system in three months, ready to feed straight into the website rebuild
- Clear the accessibility failures and hit WCAG AA (the standard for accessible contrast and interaction)
- Create one source of truth that lines designers and developers up
- Keep the existing brand feel so a loyal, decade-old audience doesn't feel alienated
- Future-proof it with dark mode and rebranding flexibility, including the sister site Racenet

## Outcomes

- **Designer productivity up 30%.** Work that took a week got done in 3 to 4 days
- **Contrast errors all but gone.** WCAG AA across almost every component
- **Accessibility built in by default:** touch target sizes, focus states, alt text, screen-reader support, all encoded rather than checked by hand
- Developers stopped hunting through design files for CSS properties, because the values lived in the tokens
- Months saved on the Racenet sister-site rebrand, since the architecture was built to reskin
- Full documentation, so new people could onboard on their own

## Process

### Discover

**I audited everything before designing anything: the Sketch files, the live site, and the CSS codebase.** I wanted the real scope, not a guess. It confirmed the picture end to end: no cohesive colour palette, no icon library, no type scale, no spacing system, no grid, and 1,450+ contrast errors across six pages. That audit became the brief. Every decision after it traced back to a specific problem I'd found.

### Develop

**I chose the architecture for scale, not speed.** Token Studio (a Figma plugin for managing design tokens) for the token setup, and Brad Frost's Atomic Design method for structure, building from the smallest pieces up so the system stays consistent as it grows. Tokens split into global (raw values) and semantic (values with a job, like "surface" or "danger"), which is what lets a brand or theme change cascade cleanly.

**I kept the brand, rebuilt the foundations underneath it.** I held onto the existing Roboto typeface so the loyal audience wouldn't feel jolted, then built a proper 1.2 Minor Third type scale, sized larger on desktop than mobile, with a separate iOS token set using SF Pro so it felt native on Apple devices. I set the Material icon library as the default to kill the mismatched icon styles.

**Colour was rebuilt in Leonardo Color so accessibility was structural, not manual.** Leonardo generates colour scales that hold their contrast ratios, so I could bake WCAG AA into the palette itself: as a rule, a token at level 600 or above paired with 50 or below passes reliably, which gave designers a safe default instead of a contrast checker and a guess. I generated a dark mode palette alongside it, from the same token structure.

**I built components atoms-up, against real pages.** Buttons, inputs, checkboxes, breadcrumbs, dropdowns, and forms came first, each one tested on an actual page rather than in isolation, so it earned its place before it went in the library. I deliberately stopped at semantic tokens rather than going all the way down to component-level tokens. With three months on the clock, going deeper would have cost real time for a level of granularity the team didn't need yet. That was a scope call, and it was the right one.

### Launch and test

**A system nobody adopts is just a folder.** I wrote full documentation covering colour usage, typography, component behaviour, interaction patterns, and the reasoning behind each decision, so the "why" travelled with the "what". Then I led adoption across the 15 people who'd be using it, walking them through the system and showing the efficiency gains early so it actually stuck rather than sitting unused.

## Learnings

**Constraints sharpen decisions.** Stopping at semantic tokens was the right call for the timeline, not a compromise. Knowing where to stop is part of the skill.

**Accessibility is a systems problem.** Encoding it into the token architecture made compliance automatic, instead of a manual fix chasing 1,450+ separate errors.

**Tokens need translation across platforms.** Converting the tokens into Compose-compatible formats for the Android developers was more complex than I expected. A token system is only as useful as the formats each platform can actually consume, and that handoff deserved more planning than I first gave it.

**Documentation is design work, not overhead.** It's what makes a system transferable to people who weren't in the room when the decisions were made.

**Leading without authority is a skill.** I owned the system but not the team. Earning their trust, and showing the payoff early, mattered as much as the architecture itself.

## Next steps

Components will keep getting added as features are built, and the tokens will evolve with the brand. The priority now is discipline: keeping the documentation and governance up to date so the system doesn't drift back into the mess it replaced.
